## Test

<!-- The test that fails before the fix and passes after. -->', 'Symptom: Waffle''s weekly cutoff was set to 98% (org limit), but at 93% used the Orchestrator dashboard still showed a warning/held state as if his own override meant nothing - the tooltip correctly says "Cutoffs: 5-hour 98%, weekly 98%" while the badge/level still reacted like something was wrong, well short of that line.

![image 1](attachments/M.ORCH-T1394/2026-11-01-image-1.png)
![image 2](attachments/M.ORCH-T1394/2026-11-01-image-2.png)
![image 3](attachments/M.ORCH-T1394/2026-11-01-image-3.png)

Cause: `UsageCutoffs.Apply` already moves a member''s own weekly/5-hour *stop* line (`WeeklyReservePercent`/`PaceTargetPercent`) with their cutoff override, but never touched `OrchestratorSettings.WarnPercent`, the dashboard''s early-warning line (default 80%) that drives `BudgetStatus.Level` (badge color, "Budget warning" toasts) and, while unpaced, the scheduler''s own hold. A member who raised their cutoff well past the org-wide 80% (every member in this org has: 98, 95, 98, 98, 98 except Erin''s 75) still warned - and was held while unpaced - at 80% regardless, so the local override never actually overrode the warn/hold behavior, only the hard stop.

Fix: `UsageCutoffs.Apply` now also derives `WeeklyWarnPercent`/`FiveHourWarnPercent` per member, keeping the same point-gap to that member''s own cutoff that the org-wide default keeps to its own (90-80=10 weekly, 98-80=18 five-hour). `BudgetStatus.Level` checks each dimension against its own warn line instead of one shared `WarnPercent`. Members without an override see identical behavior to before (gap collapses to the existing flat 80%).

Test: UsageCutoffTests - a lowered cutoff lowers its own warn line (Erin: weekly 80 -> warn 70, 5h unchanged -> warn 80); a raised cutoff raises its own warn line (Waffle: weekly 98 -> warn 88) and Level is Ok at 93% used against a 98% cutoff, where it was previously Warn.');

