<!-- A checklist of what must be true for it to be done. -->', '## Story

As a person whose dashboard CLI tab strip fills up with repo tabs, I want a pinned, unclosable,
titleless first tab that shows just my Sigma (5-hour) and lambda (weekly) usage bars, so I can read
my utilization at a glance without hunting for it, and click into it for the full charts without
scrolling past the terminal pane.

Internally and in any code/XAML naming, call this the **Usage tab** -- not "Allocation tab":
`/allocate` already means ticket-claiming (M.ORCH-T560), and a second meaning for that word on the
same screen would confuse the two.

## Acceptance

- A new tab is pinned at index 0 of every login''s CLI tab strip (Terminals panel), ahead of the
  Launcher tab and every CLI tab.
- It has no close button and can''t be closed, dragged, or reordered away from index 0.
- It has no title text -- only the Sigma (5-hour) and lambda (weekly) usage bars (the same figures
  already shown in a tab''s trailing text / the login''s dashboard body).
- Clicking it shows the full 5-hour and weekly utilization charts (the same ones already on the
  login''s dashboard body) without needing to scroll past the terminal pane.
- Gap found discussing Launcher tabs (M.ORCH-T1150/T1192/T1317, ryand 2026-11-06): once this tab is
  pinned unclosable at index 0, a login is never literally at zero tabs, so the existing "closing the
  last tab reopens a Launcher" check (which triggers on zero tabs, `LauncherRespawn.Decide`''s
  `hasLiveTab`) would stop firing. Fix as part of this ticket: that zero-tabs check must count only
  closable tabs (Launcher, CLI), ignoring this pinned Usage tab, so closing everything else still
  spawns a fresh Launcher for that login.
- Tests cover: the Usage tab can''t be closed; it stays at index 0 after other tabs open, close, or
  reorder; and the last-tab-closed Launcher respawn still fires with only the Usage tab left open
  (i.e. the respawn''s "no live tab" check ignores it).');
As a player I want one Encyclopedia that explains every control and everything I have learned (lore, astronomy, commerce, factions, ships, stations), so I can look anything up in one place.

## Acceptance
- [ ] An Encyclopedia screen (its own rebindable key, plus entries in the drawer, the pause menu and Help) with categories: Controls, Guides, Lore, Astronomy, Commerce, Factions, Ships, Stations, Settlements, Systems, Pilots.
- [ ] Controls articles are generated from Input/KeyBindings (current bindings, so rebinds show), the gamepad map and ControlsHelp: one article per action group, each with keywords.
- [ ] Guides come from the Core GameManual sections, Lore from the LoreLibrary (Prose texts and books), Astronomy from bodies and stars, Commerce from goods, industries and markets, and Factions from factions.
- [ ] Knowledge is collected: guides and controls are always known; entity articles unlock when the player discovers, scans or visits them (reuse DiscoveryLog, MarketIntel and met pilots). Unlocks are saved per game as optional fields; locked articles stay hidden.
- [ ] An open-text filter: typing "pilot" lists every unlocked article with "pilot" in its title, body or keywords (case-insensitive), with title matches ranked first and the match highlighted.
- [ ] Articles have stable ids (category/slug, from LoreKeys where one exists) and link to each other by keyword; clicking a link opens that article. Back/forward history.
- [ ] The Core model (articles, index, search) lives in SpaceGodCore with no UI dependency. It is display-only, never touches the simulation, and gives every co-op peer the same unlocks from shared state.');
As a player scanning a ship, station, planet or settlement, I want its card to show keyword links straight into the Encyclopedia, and its encyclopedia entry to show the same live readings as the scan card.

## Acceptance
- [ ] The scan/selection card (Core/Lore/ScanTabs and the HUD selection card) shows a row of keyword links: the entity itself, its faction, class or hull, body type, star, goods it trades, and notable features. Each opens the matching Encyclopedia article; the entity link opens its own entry.
- [ ] Scanning unlocks the entity entry and its linked keyword articles.
- [ ] An entity entry (planet, moon, star, ship, station, settlement, pilot) embeds the live scan stats, built from the same code as the card so they always match and refresh while open, plus its Prose description and history.
- [ ] Entries for entities no longer in sight show the last known readings and how old they are.
- [ ] Anywhere an entity is shown (discoveries screen Shift+O, system and galaxy maps), an Encyclopedia link is offered.');

As a teammate (Jordan, pdftransferjr@mindattic.com, already in the MindAttic Claude Team), I join the owner''s
tailnet with Tailscale, open the Orchestrator on my phone''s browser, and drive my own Claude CLIs on the owner''s
Windows machine: start one in a repo, type or dictate (`/voice`-style mic button) into it, see usage and the repo''s
ticket queue, and add my own Claude login(s) myself. All the RAM is on the machine in the living room; I talk to it
from bed.

Setup reference: the owner''s Tailscale + Remote Desktop guide (Downloads\Tailscale-Remote-Access.pdf). Attached to
this ticket as the source of the network half.

## Design

- The gateway (M.ORCH-T933, HTTPS :7443, already Tailscale-ready per docs/REMOTE_HOST.md) serves a mobile web app at
  `/app/`, built from MindAttic.Components web components (no one-off widgets).
- Sign-in: the owner runs `orchestrator gateway invite <who>`, which mints that member''s token and prints a link
  `https://<tailscale-name>:7443/app/#token=...` (fragment: never sent to the server or logged). The app keeps it on
  the device.
- Browser WebSockets can''t send an Authorization header, so a stream is opened with a single-use, 30-second stream
  ticket from `POST /sessions/{id}/ticket` (bearer-authenticated). A token never goes in a URL.
- A member always sees, and may interact with, sessions on their own Claude login(s); other sessions keep today''s
  rules (broadcast sharing + grants).
- Member endpoints: `GET /me` (who, logins, usage), `POST /me/sessions` (start a CLI on my login in a repo),
  `POST /me/logins` (start the Claude sign-in flow for my login as a session I drive), `GET /repos`,
  `GET /repos/{repo}/tickets` (read-only queue).
- Docs: `docs/TAILSCALE.md`, a step-by-step for the owner (share the machine) and the friend (install Tailscale,
  open the link, add accounts).

## Acceptance

- [ ] `orchestrator gateway invite jordan` prints a working phone link.
- [ ] The app works at 360px wide: sessions list, live terminal with quick keys + mic, start CLI, usage, queue,
      add login.
- [ ] UI built with MindAttic.Components (Button, Textbox, Select, Modal, Tag, ProgressBar, Toolbar, ...).
- [ ] Tokens never in a URL sent to the server; stream tickets are single-use.
- [ ] Builds clean.', '## Story

A new user (first: Jordan, pdftransferjr@mindattic.com, already in the MindAttic Claude Team) downloads
MindAttic.Orchestrator onto their own Windows 11 PC, runs it, and is walked through signing in with a proper Claude
account if they have none yet. Later, from bed, they reach that PC from their phone with Tailscale + Remote Desktop
(Microsoft''s Windows App) and the Orchestrator pivots to a phone layout: the embedded CLI fills the screen, with big
touch keys and a mic button, so they talk to the CLI instead of typing. All the RAM is the PC''s.

Tailscale is only the network path to Windows 11 (owner''s guide: Downloads\Tailscale-Remote-Access.pdf, attached).

## Scope

1. **First run / account login**: a fresh install with no Claude login signed in shows a sign-in step in the
   dashboard (who you are, sign in to your Claude account, done) instead of an empty dashboard.
2. **Responsive dashboard**: when the window is narrow or portrait (an RDP session sized to a phone), the WPF
   dashboard switches to a compact layout: one CLI full screen, a session switcher, large touch buttons (Esc, Tab,
   arrows, Ctrl+C, Enter, Shift+Tab), and a voice button that dictates into the CLI. Widening the window switches back.
3. **Docs**: `docs/TAILSCALE.md`: install, sign in, Tailscale + RDP from a phone, adapted from the PDF.

All new UI is built from MindAttic.Components (WPF), improving the library where a control is missing.

## Acceptance

- [ ] A fresh profile with no Claude login gets the sign-in step; signing in lands on the normal dashboard.
- [ ] Below the narrow breakpoint the dashboard pivots to the phone layout; above it, the desktop layout is unchanged.
- [ ] Voice button dictates into the active CLI.
- [ ] New controls come from MindAttic.Components.Wpf.
- [ ] Builds clean.');

## Context

A NexusMods commenter asked whether the editor follows mods that change the game: Pilot Overhaul''s higher
skill caps, and the extra pilot traits and inventory items from mods such as YAEC-R, YAW-CE and Additional
Vanilla Traits and Academies (which JJ''s editor mines with Scarab). Building each mod into the editor would
fill the code base with per-mod branches and risk vanilla behavior. The owner wants mod support to be
possible but **completely separate**: with no add-ons, or with a mod absent or disabled, the editor must
behave exactly like today''s vanilla 2.0.0.

Earlier attempts (all reverted, but reusable as source material):
- `ef281e3` read mod weapons/equipment/ammo/traits straight from enabled mods'' paks (`ModAssetReader`,
  `AssetLabel`); proven against the whole base pak (1003 items, 241 traits, exact hardpoints).
- `d880091` read the skill cap from the game''s `PilotSkillLevelCurve`; `CookedPackage.ReadCurveKeys`.
- `c60cc93` the `IPilotRules` seam, vanilla cap safety and a Pilot Overhaul detector (8 tests).
- Pilot Overhaul - Eternal 8.7.4 findings: own `PQPilotSkillLevelCurve` (levels to 30 at 120,000 XP),
  base caps from pilot Quality in blueprint code.

## Goals

- Mod support arrives as **add-on packs**: plain JSON data files in an `Addons` folder beside the exe.
- A pack applies only while its mod is **installed (pak present), enabled in the game''s `modlist.json`,
  and a version the pack lists**. Otherwise it is ignored.
- **No add-ons = today''s behavior**, byte for byte on saves and identical in the UI.
- Core code has **no mod names and no per-mod branches**: it exposes a few extension points; packs feed data.
- Supporting a new mod = adding a file, not shipping a new editor version.
- Packs can be **generated** from a mod''s paks by a separate tool (the Scarab step, done once by whoever
  makes the pack, never by players).

## Non-goals

- No code plugins (DLLs): no executable add-ons, nothing new for NexusMods to quarantine, no API to keep
  binary-compatible.
- No modelling of a mod''s internal logic (e.g. Pilot Overhaul''s Quality-based caps): packs state limits
  and lists; the game''s own mod code still runs in-game.
- Clans is out of scope for the first version (no Clans mod ecosystem need yet); the format leaves room.

## Design

### 1. Extension points in Core (once, mod-agnostic)
`src/MechWarrior5.Mercs.SaveEditor.Core/Addons/`:
- `IPilotRules` + `VanillaPilotRules` (cap 10), from `c60cc93`. `RosterEditor.Rules` is read on every edit.
- Catalog contributions: `PilotCatalog` and `ItemCatalog` accept extra `TraitDefinition` / `ItemDefinition`
  entries (each carrying its source pack/mod), merged after the built-in lists; a rescan replaces the
  previous contributions (pattern from `ef281e3` `Apply`/`Reset`).
- Vanilla cap safety (from `c60cc93`): Max caps never lowers a cap; the setter never raises past
  `max(limit, current)`; the slider maximum covers an existing higher cap; save keeps exactly what''s there.

### 2. Pack format (`Addons/*.json`, schema-versioned)
```json
{
  "schema": 1,
  "name": "Pilot Overhaul - Eternal",
  "author": "...",
  "game": "mercenaries",
  "requires": { "modFolder": "PilotOverhaul-Eternal", "modName": "PilotOverhaul - Eternal", "versions": ["8.7."] },
  "pilotRules": { "maxSkillCap": 30 },
  "pilotTraits": [ { "asset": "PQ_IronWill", "label": "Iron Will", "description": "..." } ],
  "mechTraits":  [ ],
  "items": [ { "asset": "...", "type": "MWWeaponDataAsset", "kind": "weapon", "label": "...", "hardpoint": "E" },
             { "asset": "...", "type": "MWEquipmentDataAsset", "kind": "equipment", "label": "...", "slots": ["General_SlotType"] } ]
}
```
- Every field optional except `schema`, `name`, `requires`. Unknown fields ignored (forward compatible).
- `requires.versions` are prefixes; empty = any version (allowed but flagged in the UI).

### 3. Loader and activation (`AddonLoader`, `AddonState`)
- Reads `Addons/*.json` on startup and on Rescan, off the UI thread. A malformed pack is skipped with a
  reason shown in Settings; it can never stop the editor from starting or touch a save.
- Detection reuses the `c60cc93` detector, generalized: mod folder found by folder name or `mod.json`
  display name, pak present (following Vortex links), enabled in `modlist.json`, version matched.
- **One decision point** builds the active set: packs whose mod is active and that the user hasn''t turned
  off. Rules: highest `maxSkillCap` among active packs wins (never below vanilla); traits/items merged
  (built-in wins over a pack for the same asset; between packs, the first in load order wins, reported).
- Per-pack switch in Settings (on by default for an active pack, since the pack itself is the opt-in;
  a global "Disable all add-ons" switch forces vanilla).

### 4. UI
- Settings > **Add-ons** panel: one row per pack with its name, the mod it needs, status (active / mod not
  installed / mod disabled / version not supported / invalid pack) and an on/off switch; "Open Addons folder".
- Pickers label pack entries with their mod (a small chip, as modded ''Mechs already have).
- Pilots page cap header shows the active range (CAP (0-10) in vanilla).
- All new text in the six languages.

### 5. Pack generator (separate tool, not in the app)
`tools/AddonPack` console app (reusing `ef281e3`''s `ModAssetReader`/`AssetLabel` and `CookedPackage`):
`AddonPack <mod folder> [--name] [--out Addons/<mod>.json]` reads the mod''s paks and writes a pack with its
traits, items (hardpoints from `WeaponType`, slots from `SupportedSlotTypesAssetId`, AI-only weapons
skipped) and, when the mod ships a pilot skill curve, `pilotRules.maxSkillCap` from its top level. Output is
reviewed by a human before it''s published.

### 6. Distribution
- Packs live in the repo under `addons/` and ship inside the release zip''s `Addons` folder; players can drop
  in more. The first pack: Pilot Overhaul - Eternal (caps only, once it works with DLC 9). Then generated
  packs for YAW-CE, YAEC-R, Additional Vanilla Traits and Academies.
- Docs: user guide section "Add-ons", a `docs/ADDONS.md` pack-format reference for pack authors.

## Safety rules (must hold)
- No packs, or no active packs: the app behaves exactly as vanilla 2.0.0 (verified by the existing suite).
- A pack can only add choices and raise the cap; it never removes built-in entries or lowers limits.
- An inactive pack''s data is never offered or written. Data already in a save (from any mod) is preserved
  by the lossless serializer regardless.
- Saves with caps above the active limit are kept as they are (vanilla cap safety).
- The save-time asset check still warns when a save references something the installed game/mods lack.

## Phases
1. Core seam + vanilla cap safety (restore `c60cc93` pieces) + tests. No behavior change.
2. Pack format, loader, detection, decision point + tests (fake mod folders, malformed packs).
3. Catalog contributions + picker labels; Settings Add-ons panel; translations.
4. `tools/AddonPack` generator (restore `ef281e3` reader into the tool) + tests against the base pak.
5. First packs, docs (`docs/ADDONS.md`, user guide), release.

## Verification
- Unit: pack parsing (valid, missing fields, unknown fields, bad JSON), activation matrix (not installed /
  disabled / wrong version / active / user-off), rule merging (highest cap, never below 10), catalog merge
  precedence.
- Headless: full existing suite unchanged with an empty `Addons` folder (vanilla regression gate);
  a real-save test with a test pack active (caps up to the pack''s limit save and reload) and inactive
  (vanilla, existing higher caps kept).
- Generator: run over the base pak as if it were a mod and match the built-in catalogs (as `ef281e3` did).
- Render audit of the Settings Add-ons panel and the Pilots page; in-game check of an edited save with a
  pack active and its mod enabled.

## Risks / open questions
- Pilot Overhaul''s real per-pilot cap rules are unconfirmed (no working PO save yet); its pack states the
  curve''s top level (30).
- Mods change between versions: packs are version-gated; a stale pack stays inactive rather than guessing.
- Asset collisions between packs: first wins, reported in Settings.
- NexusMods distribution: packs are JSON inside the GitHub zip, so nothing new is quarantined.', '# Add-on packs: optional, data-only support for other mods

## Context

A NexusMods commenter asked whether the editor follows mods that change the game: Pilot Overhaul''s higher
skill caps, and the extra pilot traits and inventory items from mods such as YAEC-R, YAW-CE and Additional
Vanilla Traits and Academies (which JJ''s editor mines with Scarab). Building each mod into the editor would
fill the code base with per-mod branches and risk vanilla behavior. The owner wants mod support to be
possible but **completely separate**: with no add-ons, or with a mod absent or disabled, the editor must
behave exactly like today''s vanilla 2.0.0.

Earlier attempts (all reverted, but reusable as source material):
- `ef281e3` read mod weapons/equipment/ammo/traits straight from enabled mods'' paks (`ModAssetReader`,
