Checked from MindAttic.Orchestrator''s window (read-only, not touched): C:\Apps\MindAttic.Launcher\ has no MindAttic.Launcher.exe at all right now - only two renamed copies, MindAttic.Launcher.exe.stale-20261121041621391 and .stale-20261121200301263 (22,494,190 bytes each, so a full build, not a truncated one), plus the .pdb from the 04:16 build and a repo.path written 20:07. Looks like run.ps1''s publish-swap (rename old exe to .stale-<timestamp>, write the new one) ran twice and was interrupted before the new exe landed either time, leaving the folder with no live exe. Needs its own run.ps1 run (or -Force) from the Launcher repo to rebuild and complete the swap; worth checking why two swaps in a row both failed to land the exe (disk space, antivirus lock, a build error) rather than just rerunning once.', 'Ryan: invoking MindAttic.Launcher.exe (run.bat/shortcut) failed with PowerShell CommandNotFoundException: C:\Apps\MindAttic.Launcher\MindAttic.Launcher.exe didn''t exist.

## Mitigated (2026-11-21 20:10, from the Orchestrator window)
Copied the last good build (Nov 21 04:16, the file left as MindAttic.Launcher.exe.stale-20261121200301263) back to MindAttic.Launcher.exe; `--write-settings-manifest` exits 0. **Not durable**: the next launch while sources are mid-edit removes it again (below).

## Cause
1. The source doesn''t build right now: M.LNCH-T137 (Azure SQL backup) is mid-edit in the working tree (uncommitted; AppSettings/Project/SettingsStore changed 19:57-20:05, BackupCommand.cs still being saved at 20:10). AppSettings dropped BackupSqlInstances / BackupSqlSkipUnreachableInstances / BackupSqlLoginTimeoutSeconds and Project dropped SqlServer, but BackupCommand.cs, BackupMenu.cs and SqlBackupService.cs still use them (CS1061 x5). Expected for in-progress work; leave it to T137''s session.
2. The real bug: run.ps1''s Publish calls Move-Aside on the live exe/pdb **before** `dotnet publish`. run.bat runs run.ps1 on every launch, and Test-NeedsPublish is true whenever a .cs file is newer than the exe, so any launch during an edit renames the running exe to .stale-<stamp>, the build fails, and the folder is left with no exe. Happened at 04:16 and 20:03 today. The .stale files survive the next run''s cleanup because running host processes still hold them open.

## Fix (run.ps1 only; doesn''t touch T137''s files)
- Publish to a staging folder (`$env:TEMP\MindAttic.Launcher.publish-<guid>`) instead of `--output $deployDir`.
- Only after exit code 0 and the staged exe exists: for each staged file (recursive), Move-Aside its deployed counterpart, then copy it into C:\Apps\MindAttic.Launcher. Remove staging in a finally.
- Drop the up-front `Move-Aside $exe` / `Move-Aside $pdb` (the per-file loop covers them).
- Make the `*.stale-*` cleanup `-Recurse`, since Move-Aside can now rename files in subfolders.
A broken build then leaves the deployed exe as it was ("Deploy FAILED", existing build still launches) instead of deleting it.');

**Prose exists** at D:\Projects\MindAttic\Prose. It does **not** depend on `MindAttic.SpeechRecognition`;
its speech-to-text is a separate, self-contained implementation: `src/Prose.Core/Services/Discussion/SpeechService.cs`.

### STT providers found in Prose
- **Groq-hosted Whisper** (`whisper-large-v3-turbo`) -- Prose''s primary/live STT provider. Prose''s own code
  comment calls it "the fastest hosted transcription available", at about $0.04/hour. Batch/per-utterance
  (no streaming, no partials -- explicitly out of scope per a `writer.js` comment: "An utterance is seconds
  long; a whole dictated chapter would need streaming").
- **OpenAI Whisper** (`whisper-1`) -- Prose''s fallback only, used when Groq is absent/fails. $0.006/minute
  (~$0.36/hour), i.e. ~9x Groq''s price. Same batch/per-utterance shape.
- Both speak the identical OpenAI-compatible `multipart/form-data` "audio/transcriptions" REST endpoint, so
  Prose posts to both with one shared code path (base URL, model name and API key are the only differences).
- ElevenLabs and Piper/System.Speech also appear in Prose''s codebase but are **text-to-speech** (narration /
  read-aloud / audiobook export), not STT -- not relevant to this comparison.

### Compared to Gadfly''s current STT
Gadfly uses `MindAttic.SpeechRecognition` with `Provider=ElevenLabs` (Scribe, streaming, partials, word
timings, diarization) as the live default. That shared library **already ships an `OpenAI` provider**
(`OpenAiWhisperRecognizer`, batch/no-partials/no-diarization) as a pass-through choice for
`MindAttic:SpeechRecognition:Provider` -- already wired in `Gadfly.Server/Voice/VoiceServices.cs`, already
documented in `docs/VOICE.md`, already off by default (ElevenLabs stays the live default). No new scaffolding
was needed or added for that; it already exists.

### The credible faster alternative: Groq, not plain OpenAI
Plain OpenAI Whisper is **not** documented anywhere as faster than ElevenLabs Scribe -- Prose uses it only as
a slower fallback. The one provider with a *documented* speed claim is **Groq''s hosted Whisper**, which has
no equivalent in `MindAttic.SpeechRecognition` today. Adding it correctly (distinct `ProviderName`, its own
vault key `MindAttic:Vault:LLM:groq:apiKey`, its own options/config hooks, README/docs, tests) requires
changes inside the shared library itself, not just Gadfly''s usage of it -- so per GAD-T414''s own instructions,
that was **not** guessed at from Gadfly''s side. Filed as **SR-T12** in the `MindAttic.SpeechRecognition` repo
with full acceptance criteria (new `GroqWhisperRecognizer`/`GroqWhisperOptions`, a `"groq"` case in
`RecognizerFactory`, config keys, docs, tests -- modeled directly on the existing `OpenAiWhisperRecognizer`,
since Groq''s endpoint is OpenAI-compatible).

### What''s left for the owner to decide
- Whether to pull in SR-T12 (Groq provider in the shared library) and then add `Provider=Groq` as a third,
  still off-by-default, selectable option in Gadfly -- a real decision once GAD-T413''s timing instrumentation
  gives actual per-call latency numbers for ElevenLabs Scribe vs. alternatives in Gadfly''s own pipeline (Prose''s
  "fastest" claim is Prose''s own, unverified in Gadfly''s environment/workload).
- No Gadfly code was changed: the one real candidate (Groq) needs the shared library change first (SR-T12),
  and the only provider Gadfly could scaffold without that is OpenAI Whisper, which already exists as a
  selectable option and is not documented as faster.

Leaving this ticket open pending the owner''s read of the above and SR-T12''s resolution.');
As the owner, I can Lock a login (any real login: a per-CLI one or the global login, never the API key) so the Orchestrator can''t open a CLI on it, dispatch or move work to it, or keep its CLIs running, until I Unlock it. A login also locks itself when it reaches its own weekly cutoff; only I can unlock it (no auto-unlock, not even at the weekly reset). A locked login stays shown in the tree and tab strip with a lock badge.

## Design (from planning, 2026-11-23)
- State: a new JsonStore book `login-locks` (Core\Budget\LoginLock.cs), modelled on UsageCutoffBook/WeeklyAllowanceBook: per email, reason (manual | weekly-cutoff), when, by, the week reset it was set in, plus a log. Keyed by normalized email, so the global login is lockable too.
- Enforcement reuses the disabled-member seams (M.ORCH-T1952): AccountDirs.SignedIn() drops locked emails (covers LoginPicker, Dispatcher auto-pick, AccountRebalancer targets); AccountPacer gets a LockedPace like DisabledPace; explicit-login paths (Dispatcher.LoginOf, start --account, org start, WPF StartSessionAsync, General Prompt and Launcher auto-open, Run Launcher) refuse a locked login with why.
- Auto-lock hooks AccountRebalancer.CheckCutoffs (runs every tick, before the rebalance-off early return).
- Distinct from Disable (M.ORCH-T1958): Lock carries a reason, covers the global login, auto-sets at the weekly cutoff, blocks manual opens, and shows a badge.

## Open decision for the owner
Whether an active `org allow` (WeeklyAllowances) suppresses the weekly auto-lock. Planned default: a lock always wins over an allowance (a locked login stays locked); an allowance given while unlocked defers the auto-lock until the allowance ends. Unlocking a weekly auto-lock suppresses re-locking for the rest of that weekly window.

## Acceptance
- Right-click a login, Lock: no new CLI, dispatch or move lands on it; its CLIs are paced to stop; lock badge shows. Unlock restores it.
- A login crossing its weekly cutoff is locked within one tick and stays locked past its weekly reset until Unlock.', '## Story
As the owner, I can Lock a login (any real login: a per-CLI one or the global
login, never the API key) so the Orchestrator can''t open a CLI on it, dispatch
or move work to it, or keep its CLIs running, until I Unlock it. A login also
locks itself when it reaches its own weekly cutoff; only I can unlock it (no
auto-unlock, not even at the weekly reset). A locked login stays shown in the
tree and tab strip with a lock badge.

## Design (from planning, 2026-11-23)
- State: a new JsonStore book `login-locks` (Core\Budget\LoginLock.cs), modelled
on UsageCutoffBook/WeeklyAllowanceBook: per email, reason (manual |
weekly-cutoff), when, by, the week reset it was set in, plus a log. Keyed by
normalized email, so the global login is lockable too.
- Enforcement reuses the disabled-member seams (M.ORCH-T1952):
AccountDirs.SignedIn() drops locked emails (covers LoginPicker, Dispatcher
auto-pick, AccountRebalancer targets); AccountPacer gets a LockedPace like
DisabledPace; explicit-login paths (Dispatcher.LoginOf, start --account, org
start, WPF StartSessionAsync, General Prompt and Launcher auto-open, Run
Launcher) refuse a locked login with why.
- Auto-lock hooks AccountRebalancer.CheckCutoffs (runs every tick, before the
rebalance-off early return).
- Distinct from Disable (M.ORCH-T1958): Lock carries a reason, covers the global
login, auto-sets at the weekly cutoff, blocks manual opens, and shows a badge.

## Decision (resolved by the owner, 2026-11-23)
A lock always wins over an active `org allow` (WeeklyAllowances): the weekly
auto-lock fires at the login''s cutoff regardless of any allowance in effect, so
reaching 100% of the week stays impossible. (An allowance still relaxes pacing
below the cutoff as it always has; it just can''t hold off the lock once the
cutoff is reached.) Unlocking a weekly auto-lock suppresses re-locking for the
rest of that weekly window.

## Acceptance
- Right-click a login, Lock: no new CLI, dispatch or move lands on it; its CLIs
are paced to stop; lock badge shows. Unlock restores it.
- A login crossing its weekly cutoff is locked within one tick — even with an
active org allow — and stays locked past its weekly reset until Unlock.');
reason weekly-cutoff and the week''s reset when the week reading is at or past
the login''s own weekly cutoff, unless already locked or the owner unlocked it
in this same weekly window; never unlocks (not even at the reset). Called each
tick from AccountRebalancer.CheckCutoffs
(src\MindAttic.Orchestrator.Core\Sessions\AccountRebalancer.cs) for every
signed-in login and the global one, before the rebalance-off early return.

Resolved (epic M.ORCH-T2108, 2026-11-23): a lock always wins over an active
`org allow` — AutoLock does NOT take WeeklyAllowances into account and fires at
the cutoff regardless of any allowance, since the whole point is that reaching
100% of the week must stay impossible. Drop the `allowedPastWeek` parameter
from the signature above.

Outcome: tests pass for lock-at-cutoff (with and without an active allowance),
no-relock-after-unlock-same-week, still-locked-after-reset.');
Files:
- tools/fetch-piper.ps1 (new). Downloads the pinned Piper release (rhasspy/piper tag 2023.11.14-2, piper_windows_amd64.zip, MIT; NOT piper1-gpl) and the voice en_US-libritts_r-medium (.onnx and .onnx.json from huggingface rhasspy/piper-voices, at a pinned revision, CC BY 4.0) into Vendor/Piper/. It checks each file''s SHA256, is idempotent, and takes -Force to fetch again. Running it once to record the hashes is allowed.
- .gitignore: the binaries and models under Vendor/Piper/ are not committed; the script is.
- Vendor/README.md: a Piper row giving the version, licences (Piper MIT; espeak-ng-data GPL-3, shipped inside Piper''s own folder and used only by piper.exe, never linked by the game; the voice CC BY 4.0 with its attribution) and how to refresh.
- tools/package.ps1: stages Vendor/Piper next to the packaged executable (for example <exe dir>/Piper) when it is present, and says so when it is not, since the game then falls back to Windows SAPI.
Outcome: the script parses (powershell -NoProfile -Command "$null = [scriptblock]::Create((Get-Content -Raw tools/fetch-piper.ps1))") and package.ps1 still parses the same way. No C++ changes, so no editor build is needed.


Verification is build-only: the editor build succeeds (tools/build.ps1). Do not run tests, smokes, the bench or the game. If Unreal is not installed on this workstation, say so instead of claiming a pass. View-local presentation: nothing here enters the simulation update, the co-op lockstep frames, persistence or the bench digests (Bench/expected-digests.json must not move), and nothing draws from the simulation random stream; scripted runs (PlayerOptions::ScriptedRun, GameAudio disabled) stay silent and unchanged. Other agents may hold uncommitted edits in this checkout: commit only your own files. Read the feature (parent) for the design before starting.', 'Repo: SpaceGod.Unreal. Area: tooling and vendoring, no C++ yet.
