Both figures are off and in the wrong direction (tab reads higher than the live meter), not just a rounding/stale-by-a-few-percent thing. Likely the tab strip (AccountRoster row / TabTrailing binding) is reading a stale row that never refreshed, or is reading the wrong account''s/wrong window''s reading, or a fallback estimate (AccountPacer/Projection) is being shown instead of Claude''s own reported percent. Given this and the ongoing /bughunt ask: check this generically across every signed-in person (Amy/Erin/Honey/Jordan/Ryan/Ultra), not just Ultra, and across both Team-seat and solo Max/Ultra accounts, since the root cause may affect any of them.');

AccountSwitch.cs:53-59, `Seat(AccountRow row)`:
```
public static string? Seat(AccountRow row) => row.Plan switch
{
    null => null,
    var p when !p.StartsWith("Team") && !p.StartsWith("Enterprise") => null,
    var p when p.Contains("Max") => "premium",
    _ => "standard",
};
```
Classifies a Team/Enterprise seat as ''premium'' only if its Plan string contains the literal substring ''Max''. Since AccountRoster.PlanOf''s Oct 2026 rename (''Max 20x'' -> ''Ultra'', ''Max 5x'' stays ''Max''), a Team seat on the Ultra-equivalent (20x) tier now has Plan == ''Team · Ultra'', which does NOT contain ''Max'' -> Seat() wrongly returns ''standard'' for it (the opposite of premium) instead of ''premium''. Affects AccountSwitch.MenuLine/SeatOrPlan, i.e. the dashboard account menu''s seat label for anyone on a Team org whose tier is Ultra-equivalent. Fix: check for both ''Max'' and ''Ultra'' (or better, derive premium/standard from whether a tier was recognized at all, e.g. tier is ''Max'' or ''Ultra'', rather than substring-matching ''Max'' alone) once this file is free.');

AccountPacer.LoadSamples/SaveSamples kept each folder''s week of UsageSample readings (used by ReportedLimit.Highest/RaiseWeek to decide the 5-hour and weekly percent shown) tagged only by folder/email, never by the signed-in account - unlike AccountPacer.LoadLimits/SaveLimits, which already learned this lesson for limit hits (M.ORCH-T774: ''a re-signed folder would keep reading the previous account''s''). ReportedLimit.Highest/RaiseWeek''s whole premise is ''usage within a window never falls, so the higher reading is the truer one'' (it exists to catch an idle CLI restamping a stale, lower percent with a fresh timestamp). That premise only holds when every sample being compared really is an earlier honest reading of the SAME account''s SAME window. It breaks the moment a folder''s signed-in account changes (a real /login switch, or - very plausibly what happened here - the Ultra login (ryandebraal@gmail.com) was used to verify the per-CLI login model earlier (M.ORCH-T1396) and/or was upgraded Pro->Ultra today (2026-11-02, both same-day per project memory): any older, higher sample left on record under a reset-time that coincidentally still matches today''s window gets blindly trusted over a fresh, genuinely-lower, correct report, and sticks there as ''the truth'' for the rest of the window because nothing can ever un-raise it.

Fix (src/MindAttic.Orchestrator.Core/Budget/AccountPacer.cs):
- LoadSamples/SaveSamples now tag the persisted file with the account they were kept for (StoredSamples, same shape as the existing StoredLimits), and LoadSamples discards them when asked for a different account - exactly mirroring LoadLimits.
- AccountPacer.Tick also drops the in-memory State.Samples cache the moment a tick sees the folder''s account change, so a long-running process (not just a fresh read from disk) stops trusting the old account''s samples immediately, not just after a restart.
- This is account-agnostic: it protects every signed-in person the same way, Team seat or solo Max/Ultra, whoever''s folder gets re-signed to a different account.

Tests added (tests/MindAttic.Orchestrator.Tests/AccountDirsTests.cs):
- LoadSamples_discards_a_previous_accounts_samples_once_a_different_account_is_asked_for - direct unit test of the new tag/discard behavior.
- A_resigned_folders_fresh_low_week_is_never_raised_to_the_previous_accounts_higher_one - end-to-end through AccountPacer.Tick: acct-old reports 80% of a week resetting in 3 days; the SAME folder re-signs to acct-new, which genuinely reports 2% of a week with the identical reset timestamp. Before the fix this reproduces the bug (acct-new''s Budget.WeeklyPercent comes back raised to 80); after the fix it stays at 2, and the persisted samples file is confirmed re-tagged to acct-new.

