The SET CLI (login waffle@mindattic.com, account 278b4195…/96142256…, session 425ad1d0…, CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR set, so it reads its own `rl-status.json`) was told by the UserPromptSubmit note at about 03:50Z on 2026-11-03: "Claude reports the window at 96%, 2 points under the 98% stop: no more than 0 subagents at once." Five minutes later every tool call was refused at the stop, and the window went to 100%. The 98% target is meant to be unreachable.

## Evidence (orchestrator.db, read-only)

`gate_events` for this account:

| at (Z) | decision | held s | tool | percent |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 03:34:44 | held | 25.0 | PowerShell | 89.0 |
| 03:55:25 | refused | 300.1 | Edit | 99.0 |
| 04:00:26 … 04:25:29 | refused ×6 | 300 | Edit/Bash | 100.0 |
| 04:31:54 | prompt-answered | | | 100.0 |

So the gate saw 89 → (96 at the prompt note) → 99 → 100 with nothing held in between: the first reading at or past 98 was already 99, and the window then hit 100 while the CLI was refused (a subagent had just finished; the main session''s own calls were what was refused).

Why the stop could not land early:

1. **`usage_samples` has no rows after 2026-10-22T22:03Z.** The five-hour sampler has been silent for eleven days (last rows: 13→15% toward a 02:20Z reset on Oct 23). `accounts.read_at` for the Team logins is also from 2026-10-21. The daemon is therefore not sampling Claude''s meter; the percent the gate acts on comes only from statusline restamps, which are whole numbers and lag while subagents spend (the M.ORCH-T203 / M.ORCH-T1110 failure mode again).
2. **`ticket_costs` has no rows for session 425ad1d0…** (and none for SET-T25…T32), so the ledger saw none of this session''s spend. `BudgetCalculator.StopPercent` (M.ORCH-T1110) adds "tokens spent since the percent was seen" plus one minute of burn to the reported percent; with no events for this login both terms are zero and the stop degenerates to the bare reported integer. That is exactly the Oct 26 case the T1110 comment describes ("97% at 04:49Z, 99% at the next refresh").
3. **The pace note urged maximal parallelism earlier in the same window** ("up to 9 may run at once… start more now… Don''t hold one back") while reading the same blind meter; the review subagents this session ran cost 200k–400k tokens each (their own reports state 124k–398k), i.e. a point or more of the window per minute near the end.
4. **The account is not in `accounts`** (rows: pdftransferjr, shampoo1232, ryandebraal@mindattic, ohleo199), so whatever per-login sampling or cutoff bookkeeping keys off that table does not cover waffle@mindattic.com.

## Expected

With `PaceTargetPercent = 98`, a login should be held (not refused) as it approaches 98 and never see 99 or 100. `pace-gate.py` has `STOP_PERCENT = 99` as its fallback when no status names a cutoff; the status did name 98, so the fallback is not the cause, but it is worth checking that the fallback cannot be what fires when the status is stale.

## Suggested fix

- Restore the sampler for every login (including per-config-dir logins) and alarm when `usage_samples` or the account''s `read_at` is older than the tick; a "Blind" grade exists in `pace_ledger`, so surface it in the pace note instead of "coast".
- Attribute subagent and main-session spend to the per-login ledger so `StopPercent` has events to extrapolate from; until it does, treat a reported percent with no events behind it as stale and hold from (target − StopLead × control burn), not from the integer.
- Cap the subagent allowance by the distance to the target in minutes of current burn, not by a fixed 9; at 1.7M/h and ~1 point/min, a 2-point margin is two minutes.
- Record the stop in `pace_ledger` (the last rows there are from Oct 23; this window''s `limit_hit_utc` should read 2026-11-03T04:00Z).');

