1. Contract file: SpaceGod writes a versioned, schema-validated Universe Interchange snapshot (spacegod.universe.json). It is generated headlessly from Core, deterministic, with stable ids such as system/planet/ship/pilot slugs that never change between runs. It is diffable, and every export records the build hash.
2. Field ownership: simulation facts (orbits, stats, positions, cargo, histories) belong to the game and are read-only canon in Prose. Narrative fields (lore, voice, motives, rumours, technology descriptions) belong to Prose.
3. Game to Prose: Prose imports idempotently through its own Hub endpoint (--universe-import or POST /api/universes/spacegod/import). Never raw SQL, and never Prose''s UniverseGraphService paths. Use the RFC 0007 tools (get_universe_entity, search_universe, --universe-export) to read back.
4. Prose to game: Prose never edits game data. It emits proposals (a new technology, a character arc, a story template, a mystery hook) to outbox consumer ''spacegod''. A SpaceGod ingest step turns accepted proposals into versioned data files in this repo (for example Data/Lore/*.json) that Core loads deterministically. Nothing reaches the sim without being committed, so the Bench and rewind stay deterministic.
5. Verification: export, import, export again, then diff to zero, as RFC 0007 did for EVE. Add a check that the entity count in the spacegod universe matches the file, so a cross-universe leak shows up at once.
Read ExperimentEve''s own scripts/universe.mjs and its notes before building, for lessons learned.');

