Every committed HEAD from US-T137 on matches Bench/expected-digests.json, and a rebuild of the same Core gives the same digest (no non-determinism seen). BF93613840FDB047 / E2E1DE96F8CD13CC was not a committed state: it came from a run that picked up in-flight uncommitted Core work in the shared tree (which right now moves both digests again, to the values above). Nothing to bisect on main and no baseline to record. Whoever commits that sim work must bench HEAD + their change and record a baseline with its reason if it legitimately moves them.');

**Export:** `tools/universe-export.ps1` builds tools/universe-export/UniverseExport.cpp. It is a headless Core runner that links SpaceGodCore.lib and needs no Unreal. It writes the gitignored Data/Prose/spacegod.universe.json and validates it against Data/Prose/universe.schema.json (a copy of Prose''s schema).
- Inputs: seed 1977, start JD 2461324, 30 days at coarse detail.
- Ids are Prose-stable slugs.
- Runs are deterministic: two runs produced byte-identical files.
- The build hash is recorded in the `spacegod-export-manifest` entity.
- Runtime is about 17 s.

**Imported into Prose ''spacegod''** (build e9de3ba): 4823 entities and 12666 edges.
- The import went through the Hub only: POST /api/universes/spacegod/import with X-Prose-Key, in batches of 400.
- The export back used Prose''s export_universe_file through /api/mcp-invoke. The diff came back IDENTICAL.
- Snapshot check (scope=all): 3549 nodes and 12183 edges. That is exactly the file''s distinct names and relations: no foreign node, no extra or missing edge.
- A second import created 0 entities and 0 edges.
- The first single-request push lost the Hub (the process exited) part way through its edge pass. The Record-based export still diffed clean, so the leak check now compares edges too.

**HEAD a7e53b2 (US-T146 landed):** the export adds 7 gas-harvesters. I have not pushed it: the run''s history changed, so 28 pilots differ, and Prose cannot delete them (see item 1 below). Use `tools/prose-sync.ps1 -Export -AllowStale`.

**Proposed Prose changes (I did not edit Prose):**
1. Import never deletes, so re-syncs leave stale entities and edges. Proposed: a prune/replace mode.
2. The schema has no provenance block (build, seed, generator), so these ride in a manifest entity.
3. Relations are only {to, kind}: there is no weight, date or note.
4. There are no typed coordinates or units: positions live in details.
5. Import makes one SaveChanges per entity and per edge, which is slow and long-running per request.
6. The export reads relations from Record.Json, not Edges, so an import that was cut short still diffs clean.
7. The UniverseGraphService keys nodes by Slugify(Name), so same-named entities merge (4823 entities become 3549 nodes).
8. Every import request posts an outbox notice: a batched push leaves about 27 of them.
9. Edges all carry Source=interchange, whichever consumer sent them.

**Outbox:** `tools/prose-outbox.ps1` drains /api/outbox/spacegod.
- Every event is journalled to the gitignored Data/Lore/.outbox/.
- Accepted proposals (kind proposal.<category>, data = entity + status) go to Data/Lore/<category>/<id>.vN.json as pending-review. Nothing reaches the simulation.
- Tested live with -Peek and a drain, and offline with a sample proposal.

**MCP:** `.mcp.json` registers Prose.Mcp (../Prose Release build) with PROSE_UNIVERSE=spacegod. get_universe_entity(spacegod, pilot-tomas-brennan) answers via the Hub.

**Roles and Bench:** no simulation or role behaviour changed. The Bench was not affected: the exporter is a separate exe and no Core files were edited.');

- **Fighters on a capital** fly the strafing run. In Battle::Dogfight (crewed hulls, through their flight model) and FighterAI (carrier squadrons) they:
  1. burn hard for a point off the nearer end of the hull;
  2. cut the drive with flight assist off (FlyCrewed now passes `FlightAssistOff` and a roll input) and coast down the whole length, with the nose on the stretch of hull they are passing;
  3. flip onto the retrograde, burn, and line up the next pass.

  A coasting squadron lead''s wingmen coast with it. A small craft''s guns now bear on a capital anywhere along its keel (`BearingPoint`), so the raking fire really fires.
- **Fighters on fighters** do loops through the merge (half the extends end in one) and rolling breaks. Every AI small craft now banks into its turns and is drawn with its roll; until now the roll of a crewed AI craft was never drawn.
- **Corvettes, frigates and destroyers** make passes at speed, moving in and out of range on their own cycle, and their noses weave.
- **Capitals** steam across the enemy''s line broadside-on at their turn rate. They come about every ~75 s, or sooner when the face they show is worn (WeakPatches exposure > 0.35, checked every 4 s). BroadsideFacing and WeakPatches lay the bow along the course (`CourseBias` 0.06, just over the hysteresis), so the fresh broadside and its shield arrays turn in. A hit lands on the patch facing it in the rolled hull frame, so turning a face away really spares it.
- **Deliverables:** dev menu Combat > ''Start a big battle and watch''; tour stop ''Two nests of hornets'' (Flight and combat, order 35, both scales); `sg.BigBattle [game|real] shots <dir> [count=N] [quit]`. Each side fields a carrier, battleship, cruiser, 2 destroyers, frigate, 2 corvettes, 3 fighters and 2 interceptors; the fight is run on 50 s to contact and a director camera cuts between overview, strafing run and dogfight chase.
- **Bench:** every digest moved legitimately, because every scenario has battles. New baselines were taken from HEAD plus this change, run twice: sol-idle 627043C3E0EF3709, high-warp A486C3FCC334718C, economy-year 1AA18DD9314A3F3B, battle ECE9D3359FA3E2E4, galaxy-ftl 99F349A63E86F97A. HEAD alone matched the old baselines. The battle scenario now ends in 893 ticks instead of 2149 (the strafers'' guns bear on the hull), and its total CPU time is about the same.
- **Video** (first version of the code, before the coast fixes and the pre-roll): R:\MindAttic\Capture\SpaceGod.Unreal.Cpp\2026.11.06T01.19.48Z_SpaceGod.Unreal.Cpp\â€¦_Video.mp4. Screenshots a second apart are in the scratchpad''s shots\US-T164 and shots\US-T164\run2.
- **Honest verdict: not hornets yet.** At 1x the fleets open 26 km apart. In the first minute the squadrons fly as formations and the capitals barely move (0.07 km/s). A Core probe over 300 s shows only 1â€“4 strafers coasting at any moment; about half of each squadron''s craft are back at their carrier rearming (U.SPC-T518), and many fighters go for fighters rather than hulls. The chase shots do show swirling, banked small craft with tracers. To read as a swarm, the speed and thrust tuning and the rearm cadence need revisiting, which is a balance call beyond this ticket.
- **Not yet seen in game:** the final tuning (pre-roll, `MinCoastKmS`/`MaxCoastS`, squadrons skipping the run-entry approach). The Unreal build queue was jammed behind other agents'' isolated full builds. It is in the Core lib, which is built and benched.');

