**What it does.** Under `-RenderOffScreen` (or `-capture-source=frames`) the game reads back each presented backbuffer (Slate `OnBackBufferReadyToPresent` -> `FRHIGPUTextureReadback`, 4 deep, copied out only once its fence has passed) at the host''s capture fps and writes it as BGRA into a {GUID} shared-memory ring. The host (`capture host --frames {GUID}`) records it through the same CaptureEngine/tape/ffmpeg (NVENC with `hq`), session folder, markers, events and screenshots as window capture. Window capture stays the default when a window exists.

**Launch.** `tools/play.ps1 -Mode dogfight -Offscreen -Seconds 30` (also `-ExecCmds ''sg.Tour auto quit''`, `-Capture ''...''`, `-CaptureStats`). By hand: `UnrealEditor.exe Cpp/SpaceGod.uproject /Engine/Maps/Entry -game -RenderOffScreen -ResX=1600 -ResY=900 -nosound -Multiprocess -capture video hq -dogfight`. `-Multiprocess` skips the editor''s startup `UBT -Mode=ValidatePlatforms`, which otherwise waits behind every queued UE build.

**Cost** (RTX 3080 Ti, 1600x900, 60 fps feed): render thread 0.6 ms per fed frame (worst 1.2-3.7 ms), 0 skipped. Game 82-99 fps with capture vs 81-93 fps baseline (same run with no capture). The first version converted the 10-bit backbuffer on one core at about 2 ms a frame; it now runs in 8 ParallelFor bands. Grabs follow game frames, so at about 80 game fps the feed gets 52-60 frames/s; the host''s pump fills the gaps by repeating the last frame.

**Proof.** A 30.2 s, 1811-frame offscreen dogfight video, plus 4 stills that were checked: correct colours and orientation, HUD present, the fight in motion.

**Notes / follow-ups.**
- When the host''s resource valve stops capture (it did once at 432 MB free RAM; the proof used a -capture-config with minFreeMemoryMB 200), the game keeps feeding, because the protocol has no ''capture stopped'' notice. Possible upstream MindAttic.Capture change: an engine event or callback when the valve closes, so a host can tell the game.
- No upstream MindAttic.Capture change was needed: FramesCaptureHost/FrameFeedVideoSource live in the vendored host (Capture/SpaceGod.Capture). Optional upstream: ship a generic shared-memory frame-input host in MindAttic.Capture.Windows so other non-.NET engines can reuse it.
- Environment: C: has about 2 GB free, so Zen DDC answers 507 Insufficient Storage. One baseline run crashed in the DDC HttpConnectionPool thread (not capture code).
- The game shows the engine''s on-screen ''Multiple directional lights are competing...'' warning in the frames (existing).');

