Replace steps 6-7 with:

6. Push to the upstream remote (`git push`). If the branch has no upstream yet, push with `-u origin <branch>`. Surface the remote''s response - and if the push is rejected (e.g. non-fast-forward), STOP and report to the user instead of force-pushing.
   Before pushing, note the range being pushed (`git rev-parse @{u}` .. `HEAD`); step 6b needs it.
6b. **Push git subtrees too.** A repo can embed another repo as a `git subtree` (e.g. a `shared/`
   folder). Its changes only reach that other repo through `git subtree push`, so `/commit` does it
   every time; it is part of committing, not a separate cross-repo action, so no `/crossrepo` is needed.
   - Find the subtrees: the prefixes in `git log --grep=git-subtree-dir: --format=%B` lines
     `git-subtree-dir: <prefix>`, unique. None: skip this step.
   - For each prefix, check whether the pushed range touched it: `git log --oneline <old upstream>..HEAD -- <prefix>`.
     Nothing: skip that prefix.
   - Find its upstream: the URL and branch the repo''s `AGENTS.md` (or README) gives for that subtree,
     else a remote named after it. Can''t find one: don''t guess; say which prefix has unpushed changes.
   - Run `git subtree push --prefix <prefix> <url> <branch>` (it can take minutes on long histories)
     and surface the output. If it is rejected because the upstream moved on, STOP and report: the
     fix is `git subtree pull --prefix <prefix> <url> <branch> --squash`, then `/commit` again. Never
     force-push a subtree upstream, and never push to a remote the repo''s `AGENTS.md` forbids.
7. Print the commit hash + a one-line summary, and each subtree pushed (prefix -> upstream).

Also: the cross-repo edit hook (context-gate / repo-window guard) should allow `git subtree push`
from /commit without /crossrepo, since the user made it part of committing.');

**Symptom.** The author says it "should be IMPOSSIBLE to go over". The 5-hour line showed 91.6% used at about 4:37 PM against a pace line of 34%. It had been over the pace line for 89 minutes and was projected to reach 150.9% at the 7:50 PM reset (100% at 5:03 PM).

**What the hook said during the burst** (Prose session 33845eed, one bestiary campaign fanning out about 80 large subagents in waves of 10-16):
- At 22%, 35%, 46%, 53%, 57%, 75% and 80% usage the UserPromptSubmit text read "Usage is at N%, under the 90% line where pacing starts, so run at full speed: a subagent on every ticket ..., parallel subagents for independent pieces". It allowed up to 16 at once.
- Pacing only engaged around 88-90%, after the burst was already committed: "Subagents: 1 at once ... ahead of the 5-hour pace".

**Likely cause.** The gate compares usage with a fixed 90% ceiling, not with the time-proportional pace line. A burst early in the window passes as "under the line" while it runs far ahead of the pace (91.6% vs 34%). Already-running subagents also keep spending after the gate trips, so the overshoot carries past 100%.

**Expected.** Exceeding the 5-hour limit should be impossible.
- Throttle against the pace line, or against projected-at-reset, not the flat 90%.
- Count the committed spend of subagents already running.
- Shrink the allowed fan-out as the projection approaches the target.
- Never tell a session to "run at full speed" while it''s over the pace line.');

  Nov 17 18:00-23:00 | over | Peak 100% | End 100% | 8.3M uncached | At-99% 115m | Held 291 calls (966.6m total) | Refused 107 +2 typed | Why: Claude reported 100%

So the fix in M.ORCH-T1750 (restoring ThrottleAgents, not zeroing the hold while Dormant) is real and working -- it held 291 tool calls for a combined ~966 minutes and refused 107 more once at the stop, a huge increase in intervention versus the untreated bug -- but the window still landed at Claude''s own reported 100%, not the configured 96-98% target (PaceTargetPercent=96, FiveHourStopPercent=98). "At 99%" for 115 minutes before the window closed shows the stop was straddling the line for a long stretch without actually holding the number under it.

Likely cause: the same mechanism M.ORCH-T1110 already named (BudgetCalculator''s StopBlockPercent / Pacer.StopPercentOf projects between Claude''s own periodic percent readings), but even with that projection and the restored per-call hold, actual spend still crossed into 100% before the hard refuse fully choked it off. Worth checking whether the per-call hold''s MaxAgentDelaySeconds ceiling (300s) is too low once many calls are already in flight when the stop is near, and whether CapByStopHeadroom''s subagent-allowance math is keeping pace with a single CLI''s own un-sub-agented burn (this window shows only 1 CLI, not a multi-subagent burst like the Prose/T1750 incident).

Expected: a window should refuse early enough that Claude''s own meter never reads above the configured stop (98% by default), i.e. land at ~96-98%, never 100%.');

