A09-H1/H2 can''t just be registered in hypotheses.json as-is. Every existing entry there (feature/label/decisionTime) points at a column already in the standard snapshot panel (gapper snapshot/build-universe at frozen decision times); GapperTestStage''s primitives (bucket-lift, proportion-diff-clustered, wilson-ci, auc-delong, etc.) operate on those panel columns. Late-runners'' own features (m0 move start, the 11 features at m0, label y) are bespoke -- computed by GapperExploreStage.LateRunners.cs directly from bars/catalysts/splits, not from the panel, which is exactly why IP-T605 needed ~700 new lines rather than a hypotheses.json entry.

Two real options, in order of how well they fit this codebase''s existing discipline:
1. Extend the label/snapshot pipeline to compute and persist the late-runner label + 11 features as real panel columns for every split (train AND validation), the same way every other hypothesis''s inputs get there -- then A09-H1 (auc-delong, model features vs gainAtStart alone) and A09-H2 (proportion-diff-clustered or wilson-ci, y given freshCatalyst) become ordinary hypotheses.json entries GapperTestStage already knows how to run, with its existing BH/Holm correction. More work, but it''s the one-time-validation-read discipline (HoldoutLock-adjacent though validation itself isn''t cryptographically locked) this study already relies on everywhere else, and it means a future A09-style question doesn''t need its own one-off validation command again.
2. A narrow, explicitly single-use addition to LateRunnersAsync: accept split=validation behind a hard confirmation (e.g. --confirm-validation-read), run the exact same train procedure (fit + 5-fold or a simple fit+score, TBD) against validation rows once, write the result, and make a second call without a fresh explicit confirmation refuse (a crude read-once gate, weaker than HoldoutLock but better than nothing). Faster, but it''s a one-off that doesn''t generalize and needs its own care to avoid becoming a precedent for skipping the panel.

Whoever picks this up: read GapperTestStage.cs''s dispatch and one or two of its test-type implementations (Auc/ProportionDiff) before choosing, and re-read amendment-09.md''s own validation language (section ''What a pass does and does not allow'') before writing anything. This is the step that actually proves or disproves tonight''s train result, so it deserves the same care IP-T605 took, not a rushed hack.');

- Finviz Elite: confirmed on finviz.com/elite -- .50/mo or .50/yr (~.96/mo). No free Elite tier. Its export (what IP-T603''s connector scrapes) is a CSV with no documented float/short-interest API guarantee beyond what''s on the site''s own screener columns.
- Polygon (rebranded ''Massive'', massive.com, since 2026-10-30; api.polygon.io still answers): pricing page shows Stocks Advanced /mo, Financials & Ratios standalone /mo, NYSE Order Imbalances /mo, partner datasets (Benzinga/TMX/ETF Global) /mo each -- but NONE of these are labeled float or short-interest on the pricing page. IP-T603''s connector assumes /stocks/vX/float and /stocks/v1/short-interest endpoints exist; that needs verifying against Massive''s real API docs before paying for anything, since the pricing page gives no evidence those endpoints are real or which tier includes them.
- FMP (financialmodelingprep.com): pricing page blocks automated fetches (403); third-party estimates (unconfirmed) put it around -139/mo depending on tier, geography coverage split (US only vs US+UK+Canada). Needs checking by hand in a browser.

Recommendation: before paying for anything, verify Massive''s actual float/short-interest endpoints exist in their docs (not just the pricing page) since that''s the cheaper-looking option if real; otherwise Finviz Elite''s .96/mo annual is the firmest confirmed price for at least some of what IP-T603 wants.');
