- `orchestrator next --repos SpaceGod.Unreal --claim` keeps answering "the pace has no room for another subagent". At the same time this login has 0 subagent marks, its week is at ~6%, and RAM is under the guard line.
- The global login (~/.claude) is ryandebraal@mindattic.com, which is LOCKED. Its rl-status.json has subagent_allowance 0. If `next` ever plans on the global pace instead of this login''s, that alone explains the refusal.
- The gmail login has no rl-status.json at all. That is by design: RateLimitStatusWriter.Write removes the file when BlockLimitIsPeak, and an Ultra plan has no 5-hour window. So this login''s pace exists only in the daemon''s shared figures. If those are missing from the CLI''s snapshot (for example, `orchestrator org usage` just failed with a SQL post-login timeout), OnOwnLogin finds no own account and sets Pace = null.
- With Pace == null, Allocate.Next (Allocate.cs:836) falls back to MaxConcurrentSessions (6) minus plan.Queue.Count(Busy). That count covers every busy ticket in the whole workspace: the dry run shows GridGame.Unity''s CLI alone with 9 being worked, so `more` is negative. This is the M.ORCH-T660 trap, which AccountPacer.cs:277 guards against for the daemon but `next` does not when the account''s pace is absent.
- The refusal still says "the pace" even though no pace was involved.

Suggested fixes:
(1) When the CLI''s own login has no pace in the snapshot, plan it as AccountPacer does for an unpaced login: min(MaxConcurrentSessions, MaxSubagents) counted against that login''s own marks, never against workspace-wide busy tickets.
(2) Never let a locked global login''s pace stand in for a per-CLI login.
(3) Have the refusal name which branch refused (own pace, global pace, or fallback) with its numbers.');

