What the cap does: in the near-copy pass, ''ids'' is every file (any repo, not distinct repos) sharing one 6-line fingerprint window. Line 77 drops windows with ids.Count>12 before pairing, on the theory a window in that many files is boilerplate (common ctor, standard Main) rather than a copy. With ~26 repos, a snippet actually copied into 13+ repos produces exactly the same signal (one window, many ids) as true boilerplate, so it''s filtered identically and never surfacedthe worst duplication case is invisible, as filed.

Why I''m not picking a number: the cap conflates two different things under one count - (a) suppressing coincidental idiom matches (getters/setters, a standard Main) that aren''t real copies, and (b) scaling with repo count, which keeps changing (~26 today, Launcher-tagged repos grow over time). Any fixed replacement number has the identical failure mode at a different N, just moved. Also found a latent correctness wrinkle: ids.Count counts raw files, not distinct repos, so one repo with 13 internal files sharing a window hits the cap with zero cross-repo duplication; the cap''s intent is really about repo spread, not file count.

Options, no clear engineering-only winner:
1. Raise the constant (e.g. 20): same failure mode, just a different silent cutoff; doesn''t fix the file-vs-repo miscount.
2. Switch the metric to distinct repo count and cap as a fraction of repos scanned (e.g. >75% of distinct repos touched = boilerplate): scales automatically as the repo count changes, fixes the file/repo miscount, but still silently drops the >cap case and still requires picking a fraction.
3. Don''t drop windows over the cap - instead report them in a separate ''ubiquitous pattern'' bucket (shared by N/26 repos: likely boilerplate or a prime extraction candidate) so the worst case is never silently invisible, just differently labeled; changes DuplicateGroup/report shape.
4. Move the threshold to a Vault setting (per the standing ''every tunable number is a Vault setting, never a const'' convention - feedback_vault_settings_for_numbers) so Ryan can dial it live instead of us guessing a number now; doesn''t by itself resolve file-vs-repo counting or the silent-drop question.

My recommendation: do 2+3 together - key the cap on distinct repo count (fixes the real bug), and surface over-cap windows as a labelled ''ubiquitous'' finding instead of dropping them, rather than just picking a new magic number. Leaving open for Ryan''s call on the cutoff fraction/number and whether the ubiquitous-bucket reporting change is wanted, or whether a Vault-setting is preferred instead.');

