## Today
Every texture comes through `Assets/GridGame/App/Services/Art.cs` via `Resources.Load` into static caches (`cache`, `thumbs`, `owners`, `figureSprites`, `backdropSets`, ...). GG-T1193 added an LRU cap (400, trim to 320) that only drops references; nothing calls `Resources.UnloadUnusedAssets` on a schedule except `EndlessStability.cs`. GG-T1191''s endless soak saw ~1.4 MB/encounter growth and Art textures 294 -> 524 over 69 encounters.

## Want
Load by scope instead of by accident:
- **Stage scope:** entering a stage, work out the set it needs: the party''s heroes (portraits, figures, thumbs, their ability icons), every enemy in the stage and all its waves (incl. bosses, summons/spawns), the stage''s board tiles and backdrop set, status icons in use. Preload that set behind a short loading transition; on leaving the stage, release everything stage-scoped (drop Art refs, then `Resources.UnloadUnusedAssets()` and a GC) before loading the next.
- **Camp/menu scope:** screens that show non-party heroes (recruit, party picker, bestiary) load those on demand and release them when the screen closes.
- **Always resident:** UI chrome, common icons, placeholder, fonts.
- A loading transition between levels (short screen or fade with progress) while the swap happens; never a hitch mid-battle.

## Acceptance
- Over many encounters in the endless run, Art''s cached texture count and Unity texture memory return to about the same level at each stage start (no upward drift); `harness.py endless --leakprobe` shows it.
- Entering a battle never loads a texture mid-battle that the scope set should have preloaded (log a warning when it happens).
- No missing art: placeholder warnings don''t rise versus main.
- Measure texture memory before/after on Android (the tighter target).', '2026-11-26T15:53:06Z');

## Meaning
- The owner''s `Thumbnail` framing in classes.json (X, Y, Zoom; set in GridGame.Studio''s Thumbnails tab) stays the **visible, centred** view. Do not change those values: the owner is re-framing every thumbnail now, so don''t touch classes.json at all.
- `tools/make_thumbs.py` bakes a square **10% larger on every side** around the same centre (side x 1.2), padded transparent off-canvas as today. Bump `VERSION` so every thumb rebakes. Consider baking at 320 px (or keep the visible part at least 256 px) so the shown area doesn''t lose resolution.
- `Ui.PortraitThumb` (Assets/GridGame/App/UI/Ui.cs) draws the baked thumb at 120% of the host, offset -10%, so at rest it shows exactly the owner''s framing. The live-portrait fallback path must match.
- A gentle **handheld camera wobble**: a slow, small drift of the art inside the margin (never past the 10%, so no edge shows), each thumbnail on its own phase, off under Reduce Motion. One shared ticker for all thumbs, not one scheduler per element, since the hub and bestiary show 100+ at once.
- **Hub and bestiary:** a grid of 100+ cards must use baked thumbs only, never full 1024 portraits (check `ThumbMaxSize` 200 and the card sizes there). Loading them on demand and releasing them is GG-T1206''s part; this ticket makes sure the thumbs are the only thing drawn.
- `make_thumbs.py --check` passes after the rebake. Studio''s Thumbnails tab preview should show the visible window (inner area), with the margin outlined if that''s cheap.

## Order
The code can land now. After the owner finishes framing, run `python tools/make_thumbs.py --all` again (or the owner''s Studio save triggers it).', '2026-11-26T15:57:32Z');

