Needs design input from the user on: which property picks the variant (e.g. a Kind/Variant enum: Ring vs Badge), what the badge-variant''s default colors are (background + glyph color, vs MaSpinner''s current MaAccent ring), what glyph to use (e.g. a refresh/sync icon), and whether it keeps the same off-UI-thread worker-dispatcher approach the ring uses (M.COMP-T33) or can run as a simple RenderTransform+Storyboard animation since a small UI element spinning is cheaper than the ring''s continuous redraw.', '2026-10-29T03:32:12Z');

```xml
<Border ...>
    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
        <TextBlock x:Name="PART_Glyph" .../>
        <ContentPresenter VerticalAlignment="Center" RecognizesAccessKey="False" />
    </StackPanel>
</Border>
```

The StackPanel has no HorizontalAlignment set (defaults to Stretch), but a StackPanel never centers its children within its own stretched bounds - it always lays them out flush at its own origin (left edge for Orientation=Horizontal). So whenever a badge''s Width/Height are forced bigger than its content''s natural size - exactly what the Orchestrator does for its fixed 18x18 status-icon badges (Content="zzz", Content="?", or a bare MaSpinner child) - the glyph+content hug the left edge instead of sitting centered in the box.

Orchestrator used to work around this with its own TabStatusSquare style (a ControlTemplate with `<ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />` directly in a plain Border, no StackPanel) but M.ORCH-T1453 dropped it to satisfy the dashboard''s own "every XAML element is a library control or library-styled" test (tests/MindAttic.Orchestrator.Tests/LibraryOnlyXamlTests.cs), which forbids a hand-rolled ControlTemplate in app XAML. Since then every 18x18 status badge (TabIdleBadge, TabBusyBadge, TabAskingBadge, TerminalTab* equivalents, MainWindow.xaml) shows its "zzz"/"?"/spinner glyph off-center, flush left - a real user-visible regression, confirmed from a live screenshot 2026-11-08.

Fix: center the StackPanel itself (HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" on the StackPanel, or replace it with a Grid so Border/Grid centers naturally) in MaBadge''s own ControlTemplate in Theme.xaml, so every badge - pill-shaped (MaBadge default) or forced-square (MaTag + explicit Width/Height, as the Orchestrator''s status icons do) - centers its glyph+content regardless of how much extra space the box has. Add/extend MaBadgeTests to cover a badge sized larger than its content.

Once fixed and released, Orchestrator''s MainWindow.xaml badges (TabIdleBadge/TabBusyBadge/TabAskingBadge and the Terminal tab''s matching trio) need no XAML change - they already set HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" on the MaBadge element itself (which centers the badge box in its parent Grid); this ticket is about the badge''s own internal content, not its position in the Orchestrator''s layout.', '2026-11-08T18:39:55Z');

The app''s old, since-removed TabStatusSquare ControlTemplate (dropped by M.ORCH-T1453) already had the fix: its ContentPresenter set TextBlock.LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight" and TextBlock.LineHeight bound to the badge''s own FontSize, which tightens the line box to the font size instead of the font''s full ascent+descent, so VerticalAlignment=Center centers the visible glyphs instead of the whole line box.

Fix: add the same two attached properties to MaBadge''s own ContentPresenter in Theme.xaml (the one M.COMP-T170 just centered), so plain-text badge content (zzz, ?, any label) sits visually centered, not just box-centered.', '2026-11-08T19:20:46Z');

Added MaTabItem.TrailingContent (object) + TrailingContentTemplate (DataTemplate): a second template part, PART_TrailingContent, positioned exactly where PART_Trailing is (docked right of the header, and pinned to the strip''s trailing edge via the same DockPanel.Dock=Right the TabStripPlacement=Left trigger already gives PART_Trailing), collapsed while null. A consumer sets TrailingContent/TrailingContentTemplate via its ItemContainerStyle the same way it already sets Trailing.', '2026-11-08T19:23:58Z');

