User has two Claude accounts both naturally labeled ''Ryan'': ryandebraal@mindattic.com (Team org seat, premium) and ryandebraal@gmail.com (personal Ultra plan, not an org member). The ''Who''s using MindAttic.Launcher?'' prompt (WhoIsUsingPrompt.cs, OrchestratorMembers.cs) lists org members by first name + role/tier from ''orchestrator members --json'', which only resolves human attribution (tickets/billing-by-seat), not which actual Claude login (full email) a given CLI/tab is signed into. ClaudeAccountCatalog/AccountScope already read the real signed-in email (oauthAccount.emailAddress in .claude.json) for per-tab account selection, but WhoIsUsing never cross-checks against that, so the same friendly name can silently cover either of the user''s two distinct logins. Needs design discussion with user on desired resolution: auto-detect from the actually-signed-in account instead of/in addition to asking, surface the bound email in the picker, or register the personal account as its own distinct roster entry.', '2026-11-07T02:26:27Z');

Cause: WhoIsUsing.Resolve() -> DetectSignedInEmail() (MindAttic.Launcher/Services/WhoIsUsingPrompt.cs:72-79) falls back to reading oauthAccount.emailAddress from the single shared global %USERPROFILE%\.claude.json whenever CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR isn''t set. That file is mutable by anyone''s unrelated Claude Code activity on the same Windows account (a /login, a token refresh) and is not evidence of who is requesting this Launcher run. When it happens to match exactly one roster member, Resolve() silently scopes the whole Launcher session to that person with zero prompt (M.LNCH-T114''s intended skip-the-question rule), so if Amy''s sign-in is the one currently sitting in the shared global file, Ryan''s unscoped launch is silently misattributed to Amy -- every CLI tab it opens then correctly, but wrongly, carries her identity.

Fix: DetectSignedInEmail() should not trust the ambient global ~/.claude.json fallback as proof of identity when more than one roster member could be using the shared Windows login. Only auto-skip the question when CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR is explicitly set (the calling context itself already declares a specific per-person login folder); otherwise always ask via the existing WhoIsUsing.Prompt.

Test: add a WhoIsUsingTests case asserting Resolve() still asks when the signed-in email comes from the ambient/global fallback path (no CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR) even though it matches a roster member exactly.', '2026-11-07T07:29:07Z');

