## Requirements (user''s verbatim intent)

- The remote handles more requests/agents because of the extra RAM.
- The remote commits on its own as it goes (same as today).
- Tests must never block the remote writing more code.
- To achieve that: the remote isolates each unit of work (ticket/story) onto its own branch and builds each branch separately/in isolation from the others and from ongoing work.
- When a branch''s work is done, its automated test run is queued for the user''s LOCAL machine, not run on the remote.
- The local test run is what decides pass/fail; on pass it signs off and the branch is merged into main (on the remote/host side).
- Branches are used ONLY on the remote. The user''s local machine must never see or touch multiple branches -- it always stays on main, piling commits on top of each other ("I like just piling code on top of code"). Local only ever fast-forwards to the newest main once a branch has been tested and merged.

This is a deliberate, explicit carve-out from the standing global rule in mindattic-agent-standard (work on main only, no branches/worktrees) -- the carve-out applies only to the remote host side of this workflow, never to local.

## Decisions (locked 2026-11-06, by user answer)

1. **Fetching the queue:** a local Scheduler job periodically pulls the list of branches that are built and ready to test from the host (so the queue is always fresh on the local machine), but it does NOT run the tests itself. The user decides when to execute them -- a separate on-demand command runs some or all of what''s queued, whenever the user chooses.
2. **Merge sign-off:** fully automatic. A passing local test run is the sign-off; the host merges that branch into its main by itself, no extra click or approval step from the user.
3. **On local test failure:** the branch is NOT abandoned or re-ticketed. It goes back to the same agent/work unit that produced it, which keeps iterating on that same branch, without blocking any other branch''s work on the host.

## Still open (to resolve during design/build)

- How many branches can build concurrently on the host (resource limits given the larger RAM budget).
- The exact queue-fetch → on-demand-run → result-report round trip: what the local Scheduler job writes/reads, and what the on-demand command''s output looks like.
- Relationship to the existing machine host/pull/sync flow in docs/REMOTE_HOST.md and setup-host.ps1 (M.ORCH-T1473/T1479) -- this sits on top of it, same host.
- Whether "local" here means the user''s actual PC (client role per REMOTE_HOST.md) or could also be a CLI running standalone; assume the user''s PC (client role) unless told otherwise.

This replaces the earlier GPU-hosting idea (Shadow PC, cloud GPU rental) for the remote-machine initiative; that research is preserved in the SHADOW quicksave (code: silky-frogs) for reference but is no longer the active plan.', '2026-11-07T02:54:26Z');
Closing a person''s Launcher tab (the "x" in their CLI tab bar, `TerminalsViewModel.CloseCommand` on the `IsMenu` pane) currently
just reopens the same already-deployed exe (`SessionControl.OpenLauncherQuietlyAsync` → `LauncherPaneRequest` → `OrchestratorOptions.LauncherExe`,
`C:\Apps\MindAttic.Launcher\MindAttic.Launcher.exe`). MindAttic.Launcher already has a self-redeploy mechanism built for exactly
this case (`MindAttic.Launcher\run.ps1 -WaitPid <pid>`: waits for the old menu process to exit, force-rebuilds, then runs the
fresh exe — "Redeploy means ''run fresh code''").

Wire the dashboard''s Launcher-tab close into that flow: closing a Launcher tab specifically (not a regular CLI tab) should
rebuild/republish MindAttic.Launcher (the same `run.ps1 -Force` path its own in-app Redeploy uses) before reopening it, so the
reopened tab runs the newest build instead of the stale one. While the rebuild runs, show a transient "Redeploying…" placeholder
tab in that slot (swapped for the real Launcher pane once the build finishes), rather than leaving the slot empty.

A failed rebuild must not be fatal: fall back to reopening the existing (stale) deployed exe, the same way `run.bat`''s own menu
falls back on a failed `-Force` ("Redeploy failed - launching whatever is already in this folder instead.").

The Launcher tab always sorts first in a person''s CLI tab bar (`MainViewModel.Terminals.cs`: `OrderByDescending(IsMenu).ThenBy(Order)`),
so "reopens at the same tab index" means that invariant must hold through the close → redeploy (or redeploy-placeholder) →
reopen cycle: it must not land after other already-open CLI tabs at any point, including while the placeholder is shown.

