As Ryan watching the Orchestrator dashboard, when a member''s CLI is actively doing work but no
ticket has been attributed to it yet, I want the sidebar''s ticket panel to still show a card in the
same visual style as a real ticket card — just carrying a live description of what it''s doing — so
I always know what every user is up to, not just the ones with a resolved ticket ID.

## Current state (from code audit)
- Sidebar ticket cards render from `MainWindow.xaml:309-340` (`SidebarTicketArea`/`SidebarTickets`,
  template `SidebarTicketTemplate`/`SidebarTicketCard` at lines 108-124), backed by
  `UserTicketRowViewModel` in `MainViewModel.Assigned.cs:47-115`, fed by
  `ApplyUserTickets`/`SyncQueueTabs` from `Scheduler.QueueItem`.
- Empty state ("No active or pending tickets") is `MainWindow.xaml:320`, driven by `HasOpenTickets`
  (`MainViewModel.Assigned.cs:259`), true only when a real `Scheduler.QueueItem` ticket exists for
  the member.
- A live "what is it doing right now" string already exists and is just not surfaced here:
  `ToolCallText.Describe(name, inputJson)` (`MindAttic.Orchestrator.Core/Activity/ToolCallText.cs:6-10`)
  feeds `SubagentActivity.Doing`/`DoingAtUtc` (`SubagentActivity.cs:85,239`) and `AgentBoardRow`
  (`AgentBoard.cs:18-28,123`). Today it''s only consumed by abort/landing logic
  (`Scheduling/AgentAbort.cs:274,318`) and `/progress`, never rendered in the WPF sidebar.
- Ticket linkage for a running session is a heuristic, not a hard foreign key:
  `Activity/TicketAttribution.cs` (`FromPrompt`/`FromWorktree`) can come back empty even while a
  session is genuinely busy — that''s the gap this ticket covers.

## Acceptance criteria
- For a selected member, any live session (`AgentBoardRow`/`SubagentActivity`) that is active but has
  no resolved `TicketAttribution` gets one synthetic card in the sidebar''s ticket area, rendered with
  the same `SidebarTicketCard` template as a real ticket.
- The synthetic card''s title/body comes from that session''s current `Doing` text (the
  `ToolCallText.Describe` output), and updates live as `Doing` changes — no static placeholder.
- The card has no ticket ID badge (or a clearly muted "no ticket" marker) and does not wire up
  `OpenInTicketsCommand` (nothing to open).
- `HasOpenTickets`/the "No active or pending tickets" empty state accounts for these synthetic rows
  too, so it never claims nothing is happening while a CLI is visibly busy.
- Real ticket cards and their `OpenInTicketsCommand` behavior are unchanged.

## Out of scope
- Changing how `TicketAttribution` resolves tickets, or making attribution a hard FK — that''s covered
  by the companion enforcement ticket.', '2026-11-07T06:01:40Z');
MINDATTIC_AGENT.md''s GOLDEN RULE says "no edit, commit or deploy happens for work that has no
ticket," but a code audit of MindAttic.Orchestrator found nothing that actually enforces this at the
CLI-activity level:
- Ticket linkage for a running session is inferred heuristically (`Activity/TicketAttribution.cs`''s
  `FromPrompt`/`FromWorktree`), not a hard foreign key — a session can run indefinitely with zero
  ticket attributed and nothing flags it.
- The existing `PreToolUse` hook (`MindAttic.Orchestrator.Core/Setup/AgentGate.cs`, `pace-gate.py`)
  only gates on usage pacing (`rl-status.json`), not on ticket linkage.
- `Scheduling/OrphanWatch.cs` tracks dispatched tickets whose session died, which is the opposite
  direction (ticket without a live session), not the gap here (live session without a ticket).
- The commit-msg hook (`shared/hooks/commit-msg`) only catches the problem at commit time, which is
  already after code was written — too late for the GOLDEN RULE''s "before any code is written for it."

## Story
As Ryan, I want a real check — not just policy text — that flags (and ideally blocks) a CLI doing
substantive work with no ticket attributed, so "every request is a ticket" is actually true, not just
asked-for.

## Acceptance criteria
- Audit confirms exactly which activity can currently happen with no ticket attributed (first N tool
  calls of a fresh session before attribution resolves? an entire session if attribution never
  resolves? subagents spawned without a ticket argument?) and documents it.
- Decide and implement a concrete enforcement point — candidates to evaluate: extending
  `AgentGate.cs`/`pace-gate.py` to also check ticket attribution before allowing further tool calls
  past a grace window; a periodic check surfaced in the Orchestrator UI (e.g. an alert badge on a
  session with no ticket after N minutes of activity); or tightening `TicketAttribution` itself so
  more cases resolve instead of coming back empty.
- Whatever is chosen must not block legitimate early-session work (reading/exploring to figure out
  which ticket something belongs to) — only flag/block once a tool call actually edits, commits, or
  deploys with still no ticket, matching the GOLDEN RULE''s own wording.
- Document the decision and final mechanism in the repo (BIBLE.md or equivalent) so it isn''t
  re-litigated per session.

## Out of scope
- The sidebar UI display of ticket-less activity — that''s the companion UI ticket.', '2026-11-07T06:01:43Z');

Cause: MindAttic.Launcher''s MainMenuCommand ''redeploy'' case (RedeployInNewTab) sends a PaneRequest{IsMenu:true} down the Orchestrator pipe for its own replacement (run.ps1 -WaitPid -Force, which republishes then relaunches the exe itself) BEFORE the old menu process exits. MainWindow.xaml.cs''s PaneBroker.ServeAsync already opened that replacement pane (via model.OpenTerminal) by the time the old process''s own tab actually ends. But MainViewModel.Tabs.cs''s OnTabGone, on seeing any IsMenu pane end, unconditionally calls OnLauncherTabGone (M.ORCH-T1511), which runs ITS OWN independent LauncherRedeploy.Run (another run.ps1 -Force) and then reopens a THIRD pane directly via Root.OpenTerminal/control.LauncherPaneRequest -- with no check for whether a replacement is already in flight. Two run.ps1 -Force invocations run dotnet publish against the same C:\Apps\MindAttic.Launcher output directory concurrently; one''s Move-Aside/publish step can catch the exe mid-rename right as the other tries to relaunch it, producing the missing-exe error.

Fix: OnTabGone, before calling OnLauncherTabGone for an ended IsMenu pane, now checks AllTerminals.Panes for another live (!Ended) IsMenu pane already open for the same owner. If the in-app Redeploy''s replacement is already there, it returns without running a second LauncherRedeploy -- the replacement''s own run.ps1 -WaitPid handles its own rebuild and relaunch. OnLauncherTabGone''s automatic redeploy-and-reopen still runs as before for every other way a Launcher tab can end (closed by hand, crashed) where no replacement was already requested.', '2026-11-07T15:53:59Z');

