1. MainViewModel.cs:335 - a Stopwatch started in RefreshAsync and never read (found independently by 3 of 6 bughunt sweep agents). Completed it: logs a Dashboard warning past 5s instead of being silently inert.
2. TerminalsViewModel.cs''s TerminalPaneViewModel.DisposeControl() stops the control but not presumeTimer/notSentTimer/typingTimer (DispatcherTimer fields from Presume/ShowInputNotSent/ShowOtherTyping). Each is self-stopping on its own Tick, so this is bounded (worst case PresumeLife, ~60s) rather than a permanent leak, but a pane closed while one is pending keeps the disposed TerminalPaneViewModel reachable from the dispatcher''s timer queue, and the eventual tick still runs SetPresumed(null)/RaiseBadges()/PresumptionChanged against the already-closed pane. Concrete repro: press Enter in a CLI, then close its tab within ~60s.', '2026-11-08T20:24:14Z');

1. Sessions/GatewayClient.cs RunTicketsAsync - a non-object JSON response (null/array/scalar) throws InvalidOperationException from TryGetProperty, uncaught, breaking the method''s documented ''never throws but on cancellation'' contract. Added a catch, matching ListAsync''s existing pattern in the same class.
2. Tickets/OpenTicketSource.cs FromDatabase - returned [] instead of null when a first-ever read of a repo''s locked/busy ticket database failed with no prior cache, which OpenFor''s  check treats as a legitimate zero-tickets answer instead of falling back to docs/USER_STORIES.md, contradicting its own ''unknown, not none'' comment and the Unknown set''s documented contract. Fixed to return null (falls through to the same markdown fallback a repo absent from the database''s repo list already takes); updated OpenTicketSourceTests to match the corrected (and more useful) behavior.
3. Settings/ExportedState.cs Write - the file write/move had no exception handling, unlike every sibling temp-file writer in this codebase (and unlike its own Read method); a transient lock (a reader on the Launcher/statusline side) would throw uncaught at both call sites (ClaudeAccount.Save, BillingMode.Write), neither of which catches it either. Added the same IOException/UnauthorizedAccessException catch its siblings use; updated ExportedStateTests (the existing test''s Throws.Exception assertion was testing the bug).
4. Setup/MachineRole.cs Save - used a fixed ''.tmp'' filename instead of the unique-per-process pattern used everywhere else in this codebase (a latent race if two processes call Save concurrently; low likelihood, a rare manual admin operation).
5. Scheduling/DiffReview.cs RunClaude - ignored Task.WaitAll''s own return value after the process exits, so a pipe read still incomplete 10s later (a lingering grandchild holding stdout/stderr open, the same race Dispatcher.Git''s own elapsed-time math already guards against) read as empty output, indistinguishable from a review that genuinely said nothing - a review that actually succeeded (ExitCode 0) could be reported as a bare failure with no reason. Now returns an explicit message for that case.

All fixes verified: dotnet build (Core, 0 errors) plus targeted tests (OpenTicketSourceTests, ExportedStateTests, MachineRoleTests - 34 tests, all passing).', '2026-11-08T20:33:04Z');

A new user (first: Jordan, pdftransferjr@mindattic.com, already in the MindAttic Claude Team) downloads
MindAttic.Orchestrator onto their own Windows 11 PC, runs it, and is walked through signing in with a proper Claude
account if they have none yet. Later, from bed, they reach that PC from their phone with Tailscale + Remote Desktop
(Microsoft''s Windows App) and the Orchestrator pivots to a phone layout: the embedded CLI fills the screen, with big
touch keys and a mic button, so they talk to the CLI instead of typing. All the RAM is the PC''s.

Tailscale is only the network path to Windows 11 (owner''s guide: Downloads\Tailscale-Remote-Access.pdf, attached).

## Scope

1. **First run / account login**: a fresh install with no Claude login signed in shows a sign-in step in the
   dashboard (who you are, sign in to your Claude account, done) instead of an empty dashboard.
2. **Responsive dashboard**: when the window is narrow or portrait (an RDP session sized to a phone), the WPF
   dashboard switches to a compact layout: one CLI full screen, a session switcher, large touch buttons (Esc, Tab,
   arrows, Ctrl+C, Enter, Shift+Tab), and a voice button that dictates into the CLI. Widening the window switches back.
3. **Docs**: `docs/TAILSCALE.md`: install, sign in, Tailscale + RDP from a phone, adapted from the PDF.

All new UI is built from MindAttic.Components (WPF), improving the library where a control is missing.

## Acceptance

- [ ] A fresh profile with no Claude login gets the sign-in step; signing in lands on the normal dashboard.
- [ ] Below the narrow breakpoint the dashboard pivots to the phone layout; above it, the desktop layout is unchanged.
- [ ] Voice button dictates into the active CLI.
- [ ] New controls come from MindAttic.Components.Wpf.
- [ ] Builds clean.', '2026-11-10T05:24:33Z');
