**Resolved by 8a4c2e54 (CoolOff now covers many subagents under one CLI, not just many CLIs).** Root cause: Pacer.CoolOff gated its last-resort cool-off (slower new subagent starts, and the only thing that arms Scheduling.AgentAbort''s already-running-subagent backstop) purely on distinct CLI count (SubagentsByCli.Count). With one CLI running many of its own subagents - this ticket''s exact 11-subagent repro, and M.ORCH-T1810''s 8-16-subagent /allocate fan-out - that count was 1, so CoolOff never engaged and AgentAbort (which only arms once Pace.CoolOffPercent is set) never even got a chance to pull a running subagent back, leaving the per-call hold (Pacer.AgentDelay) to carry the entire load alone. That hold has a real, unavoidable ceiling: at MaxAgentDelaySeconds (300s) over a StepSeconds (20s) cadence it can only ever compress aggregate throughput by a fixed ~16x, regardless of how many subagents are actually producing that throughput; once true aggregate demand needs more compression than that (which scales with concurrent subagent count, not with the hold), no amount of per-call delay can bring it back under target before the hard stop''s own backstop catches it at the limit. Raising the hold ceiling further would only move that breaking point, not remove it - so the fix instead closed the gap that kept the concurrency-reducing backstop from ever being reached: CoolOff now gates on Math.Max(clis, pace.Subagents), so a single CLI with several of its own subagents running is recognized exactly as a login split across several CLIs already was, and AgentAbort becomes reachable for it.

Checked against the other leads this ticket raised and ruled out as live causes of the cluster below: the stale-reading/projection path (BudgetCalculator.StopPercent, M.ORCH-T1110) and Pacer.Dormant''s early full-speed phase are both still in place and behaving as designed; every window in this cluster is fully accounted for by either the pre-M.ORCH-T1750 build (hold not actually engaging at all) or this CoolOff gap (hold engaged and maxed, but the concurrency backstop never armed) - see the per-ticket notes on close.

Consolidation (2026-11-18): this is the lead ticket for a cluster of duplicate pace-ledger auto-filed tickets, all the same root cause, closed together against this commit - M.ORCH-T1802, M.ORCH-T1753, M.ORCH-T1779, M.ORCH-T1810, M.ORCH-T1760.', '2026-11-18T17:28:57Z');

