## Symptom
`orchestrator next --claim` handed out container tickets: US-T742 (level:story, open child US-T744) and US-T741 (level:feature, open child US-T742) at about 2026-11-26T10:35Z to CLI session e1bd5cdc. US-T731 (label `epic`) also shows "being worked" by that session since 11:10Z. `Snapshot.MayWork` and `Scheduler`''s queue (Scheduler.cs ~line 242) leave out `IsContainer` (Epics plus Containers), so these should never have been queued.

## Likely cause (not confirmed)
`Tickets/OpenTicketSource.cs` keeps one `fromDb` read for one database path. `FromDatabase` works out the path from each repo''s parent folder. The snapshot has repos under two roots, and each root has its own database:
- D:\Projects\MindAttic (102 repos): D:\Projects\MindAttic\MindAttic.Tickets\data\Tickets.db
- D:\Projects\GunGreenEyes (2 repos): D:\Projects\GunGreenEyes\MindAttic.Tickets\data\Tickets.db

So every refresh reads one root, throws the other root''s read away, and reads again. The published status-snapshot.json at 11:13Z showed `Approved`, `Epics`, `Containers`, `Levels`, `PartOf` and `Requires` all empty, with 19 `UnknownRepos`. Its warnings were "a read is still running after 10s, with no earlier read to stand on" and "using the read from ...", one for each root. A repo whose read isn''t back yet falls back to its USER_STORIES.md, which can list tickets the database already closed. That could also be why claims on closed tickets stayed in the "claimed with no subagent on yet" count.

## Fix direction
Keep one database read for each path (a dictionary keyed by path, plus a read in flight for each path). Union the snapshot-level sets and maps (Approved, Epics, Containers, Levels, PartOf, Requires, Priorities and the rest) across roots, so one root''s read never replaces another''s. Then check that `next` never hands out an Epics or Containers ticket.', '2026-11-26T11:20:33Z');

