As the author, I want tickets to be an infinite node graph I can see and that organises itself: every ticket has an ID, a short friendly name and a verbose description; tickets connect as parent/child, prerequisite (requires / required-by), epic-contains-child and related; Tickets draws that tree; and the queue ranks tickets by how connected they are.

Requested by the author 2026-11-05 (verbatim intent): "each of those tickets should have an ID, a friendly name, and then all the details as verbose as you want; tickets can be parents or children or prerequisites or required by or epics containing child tickets, it''s all infinite node based, which means Tickets can also draw a node tree of interconnected tickets and how they interconnect to each other, and those with multiple projects as dependencies would naturally be ranked higher, and then the amount of edges changes things; upgrade Tickets to provide this kind of visualization and self organizational skills."

## Children
- Friendly name field
- Unified node-graph link model (parent/child, requires/required-by, epic contains, cross-repo)
- Node-graph visualization in Tickets.Desktop and the read-only view
- Graph-based self-ranking of the queue
- (MindAttic.Orchestrator) side panel lists claimed tickets by ID + friendly name

## Acceptance
All children closed.', '2026-11-05T22:12:37Z');
Every ticket gets a short **friendly name** (2-6 words, e.g. "Lugger record dock words") separate from the one-line summary and the markdown description. Today the summary is a long sentence and is the only label, so lists and panels show walls of text.

## Acceptance
- New `Name` column (migration; history-versioned like the rest of the row).
- `tickets ticket new --name "<friendly name>"`; `tickets ticket correct <ID> --name` to set/change it; recorded in history.
- `ticket list`, `queue`, `show`, `graph`, Tickets.Desktop and the read-only view display `ID · Name` and fall back to a truncated summary when Name is empty.
- Backfill: a one-time command that proposes a Name for every open ticket from its summary (shown for review, written only on confirm) — no silent bulk rewrite.
- `orchestrator next` prints the name in its `=== ID (repo, type): ...` header.', '2026-11-05T22:12:39Z');
Make ticket links one general, unlimited node graph. Today `ticket link` knows requires | related | follow-up-of | supersedes | duplicates | implements | part-of, and epics are a separate table for stories. The author wants: parent/child, prerequisite (requires) and its inverse (required-by), epic-contains-child, at any depth, across repos.

## Acceptance
- A ticket can be a parent of any number of children, at any depth (no fixed epic/story/ticket levels); `part-of` becomes the child->parent edge, and "epic" is just a ticket with children (optionally typed `epic`).
- Every edge has a stored direction and a derived inverse that all commands show (A requires B ⇔ B required-by A; A part-of B ⇔ B parent-of A).
- Edges may cross repos (PRS-T1 requires M.ORCH-T5), and are validated: no cycles in requires/part-of (refused with the cycle path), dangling IDs refused.
- `tickets ticket show` lists parents, children, prerequisites, required-by and related, each as `ID · Name · status`.
- `tickets ticket verify` checks the graph (cycles, dangling, closed parent with open children).', '2026-11-05T22:12:41Z');
Tickets draws the node tree: how tickets interconnect, across repos.

## Acceptance
- Tickets.Desktop (and the read-only MindAttic.Browser view) gets a Graph view: nodes = tickets (label `ID · Name`, coloured by repo, shape/badge by status/type), edges styled per kind (requires = solid arrow, part-of = containment/tree, related = dashed).
- Focus a ticket to see its neighbourhood N hops out; expand/collapse children; filter by repo, status, label, kind; open a node''s details on click.
- Layout: hierarchical for part-of trees, force-directed for the rest; stable between refreshes.
- Node size reflects its rank weight (see the ranking ticket) so hubs stand out.
- `tickets ticket graph <ID> --json` already exists — reuse/extend it as the data source; add `--format dot|mermaid` for export.', '2026-11-05T22:12:42Z');
The queue organises itself from the graph: tickets many others depend on — especially from several projects — rank higher, and the number and kind of edges moves the order.

## Acceptance
- A computed `graph weight` per open ticket: counts of open tickets transitively blocked by it (required-by closure), weighted up per distinct repo among them; children/parent edges and related edges add smaller weights; closed dependents count zero.
- Queue order = explicit priority first (author''s word wins), then graph weight, then the existing smallest-estimate rule. `tickets ticket queue` shows the weight and a one-line "why" (e.g. "unblocks 7 tickets in 3 repos").
- `orchestrator next` uses the same order, so a cross-project blocker is dispatched first.
- Weights recompute on every link/close change (no stale cache); documented formula, deterministic, unit-tested on small graphs.
- Suggestions only, never automatic edits: `tickets ticket suggest-links` may propose likely parent/duplicate/requires edges from similar summaries for the author to accept.', '2026-11-05T22:12:44Z');

