Investigated MindAttic.Avatar.AspNetCore today (AvatarAspNetCore.AddMindAtticAvatar, AvatarSpeechService, AvatarSpeechHub, AvatarSpeechSessions, ElevenLabsTtsClient): ElevenLabsOptions.ModelId exists and Gadfly already pins it (Gadfly:Voice:TtsModel, GAD-T303, default eleven_flash_v2_5). But:

1. ElevenLabsOptions is bound once from configuration into a singleton at startup (services.AddSingleton(sp => sp.GetRequiredService<IOptions<ElevenLabsOptions>>().Value) in AddMindAtticAvatar) and never re-read: a config change needs a full process restart, unlike a DB-backed per-tenant setting a consuming app could otherwise read live.
2. Unlike voiceId -- which AvatarSpeechHub.Speak(text, voiceId, cache), AvatarSpeechSessions.Start and AvatarSpeechService.StreamAsync(text, voiceId, cacheable, ct) all already thread through as a per-call override -- there is no equivalent modelId parameter anywhere in that call chain; ElevenLabsTtsClient.StreamAsync and its TtsRequest always use the frozen options.ModelId. So two organizations'' voice exams running concurrently on one Gadfly.Server process cannot use different ElevenLabs model tiers at the same time.

Wanted: thread an optional modelId through AvatarSpeechHub.Speak -> AvatarSpeechSessions.Start -> AvatarSpeechService.StreamAsync -> ElevenLabsTtsClient.StreamAsync/TtsRequest (model_id), falling back to ElevenLabsOptions.ModelId exactly the way voiceId already falls back to ElevenLabsOptions.VoiceId; include the model id in SpeechCache.KeyFor (it already takes it) so two tiers cache separately. Consider also exposing ElevenLabsOptions via IOptionsMonitor<ElevenLabsOptions> (or similar) instead of eagerly snapshotting IOptions<T>.Value into a singleton, so a config reload can change the default tier without a restart. A client-settable modelId would need the same kind of allow-list the hub already has for voiceId (AvatarHubOptions.AllowClientVoiceId) so a browser can''t pick an arbitrary model; a server-set per-call override (e.g. from Gadfly''s own per-organization setting, passed by the server, not the browser) does not.

Once this lands, Gadfly can add a real per-organization (or per-exam) TTS tier picker in OrgSettings.razor next to the EU voice-measures toggle (GAD-T259) and the setting in docs/VOICE.md''s ''Voice quality vs. latency'' section stops being a known limitation.', '2026-11-22T07:25:28Z');

