## Command shape

/alias <type> <real-name> <alias>
- type: the kind of thing being aliased. Only ''handoff'' exists today (covers both /quicksave and /quickload); the type parameter exists so this can extend to other kinds later without a redesign, but build only ''handoff'' now -- don''t invent other types speculatively.
- real-name: for type handoff, the handoff''s real shareable code (e.g. silky-frogs).
- alias: the human nickname to attach (e.g. SHADOW).

## Behavior (type handoff)

- Store the alias as a real field (e.g. alias) on that handoff''s own registry file, %LOCALAPPDATA%\MindAttic\handoffs\<code>.json -- not as a heuristic parsed from task text. This replaces the leading-caps-word heuristic MAT-T109 was going to use.
- Validate real-name is an unconsumed handoff in the registry before setting; if not found, say so and list nothing silently.
- Reject (or warn and ask) if the alias is already used by a different unconsumed handoff -- aliases should stay unique the same way shareable codes are, so /quickload <alias> is unambiguous.
- Can be run again on the same code to rename/replace its alias (last write wins), and presumably to clear it (empty alias or a --clear flag -- left to the builder).
- Likely implemented as a new prose-agent.ps1 subcommand (''alias''), mirroring ''load''/''save'', plus a new shared/commands/alias.md global command source (this repo, MindAttic.Agency, is canonical; syncs to mindattic-agent-standard/shared/commands/ then to ~/.claude/commands/alias.md via share-scripts.ps1/install.ps1 -- same pattern as quickload.md).

## Downstream effects (coordinate with MAT-T109, linked as a prerequisite of it)

- /quickload <alias> should resolve an alias the same way it resolves a shareable code today (try alias first or code first, either order, but both must work).
- The planned /handoffs brochure (MAT-T109) should display each handoff''s alias field directly instead of (or in addition to) parsing the task text''s leading word.
- /quicksave NAME should keep working, but ideally by calling the same alias-setting mechanism right after saving (so there is one source of truth for a handoff''s nickname, the alias field, not two different code paths that can disagree). Backfill: once built, set alias=SHADOW on silky-frogs and alias=REMOTE on swift-gophers (today''s two named saves, currently only named via the task-text heuristic) so they show correctly without relying on that heuristic.', '2026-11-07T04:47:13Z');
`shared/compute-mod-hash.ps1 -Path <dir>` with no `-ExcludeNames` (or `-ExcludeNames @()`) throws:
`Exception calling ".ctor" with "2" argument(s): "Value cannot be null. Parameter name: collection"` (seen 2026-11-17 publishing Mods.MechWarrior5.SaveEditor 6.0.0, whose payload has nothing to exclude).

## Cause
Lines 43-44 build the HashSet from `[string[]]($ExcludeNames | ForEach-Object { $_.ToLowerInvariant() })`. An empty array piped through ForEach-Object yields $null, so the HashSet constructor gets a null collection.

## Fix
Build the set without the pipeline result being null, e.g. start from an empty `HashSet[string]` (case-insensitive comparer) and add each name, or wrap the pipeline in `@(...)`.

## Check
`compute-mod-hash.ps1 -Path <dir>` with no `-ExcludeNames` returns the same hash as with a never-matching name (`-ExcludeNames ''__no_such_file__''`, the workaround used until now).', '2026-11-18T00:06:58Z');

